{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00072","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00072","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00072","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry Jingchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Terry Jingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gopal","familyName":"Dev","name":"Dev, Gopal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Max","familyName":"Obreiter","name":"Obreiter, Max","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Punya Syon","familyName":"Pandey","name":"Pandey, Punya Syon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keenan","familyName":"Samway","name":"Samway, Keenan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenyuan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Wenyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinya","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Yinya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bernhard","familyName":"Schölkopf","name":"Schölkopf, Bernhard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mrinmaya","familyName":"Sachan","name":"Sachan, Mrinmaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijing","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Test of Time: Rethinking Temporal Signal of Benchmark Contamination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-26T16:41:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:01:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-06T14:10:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:35:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-26T19:00:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-28T01:05:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-13T04:56:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-14T00:35:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Post-cutoff performance decay of LLMs has been widely interpreted as a temporal signal for benchmark contamination, where public information released before the training cutoff may have been included into training corpora and inflated model performance by memorization. We critically examine this view and demonstrate that this temporal signal is highly sensitive to how benchmark questions are constructed, even if the underlying source material remains invariant. Specifically, we show that LLM-transformed questions can produce remarkably different temporal patterns compared to fill-in-the-blank (cloze) questions directly retrieved from the very same documents. We validate this effect on prior benchmarks that report clear post-cutoff decay (LiveCodeBench), and show that a simple LLM-driven transformation of the same problems can effectively remove the temporal pattern. We further provide a mechanistic understanding of this phenomenon using influence function analysis. Overall, our results suggest that post-cutoff performance decay is a sensitive contamination signal, motivating more robust contamination probes for reliable LLM evaluation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00072","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:23:49Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:23:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-14T02:49:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04467","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.04467","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.04467","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengsi","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Mengsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingrun","familyName":"Xing","name":"Xing, Xingrun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulong","familyName":"Ao","name":"Ao, Yulong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghua","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yonghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PDTrim: Targeted Pruning for Prefill-Decode Disaggregation in Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-29T02:29:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-08T00:00:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-22T14:45:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T01:39:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-23T08:31:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-09-24T00:41:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-14T14:46:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-12-16T01:57:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate exceptional capabilities across various tasks, but their deployment is constrained by high computational and memory costs. Model pruning provides an effective means to alleviate these demands. However, existing methods often ignore the characteristics of prefill-decode (PD) disaggregation in practice. In this paper, we propose a pruning method that is highly integrated with PD disaggregation, enabling more precise pruning of blocks. Our approach constructs pruning and distillation sets to perform iterative block removal, obtaining better pruning solutions. Moreover, we analyze the pruning sensitivity of the prefill and decode stages and identify removable blocks specific to each stage, making it well suited for PD disaggregation deployment. Extensive experiments demonstrate our approach consistently achieves strong performance in both PD disaggregation and PD unified (non-PD disaggregation) settings, and can also be extended to other non-block pruning methods. Under the same settings, our method achieves improved performance and faster inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Minor revisions"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.04467","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-08T01:25:39Z","registered":"2025-09-08T01:25:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-16T04:48:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00088","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00088","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00088","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ting-Chun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ting-Chun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ching-Yu","familyName":"Hsu","name":"Hsu, Ching-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan-Yi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kuan-Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi-An","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Chi-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hung-yi","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hung-yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AEGIS : Automated Co-Evolutionary Framework for Guarding Prompt Injections Schema"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-27T12:25:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:02:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T04:58:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:28:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prompt injection attacks pose a significant challenge to the safe deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) in real-world applications. While prompt-based detection offers a lightweight and interpretable defense strategy, its effectiveness has been hindered by the need for manual prompt engineering. To address this issue, we propose AEGIS , an Automated co-Evolutionary framework for Guarding prompt Injections Schema. Both attack and defense prompts are iteratively optimized against each other using a gradient-like natural language prompt optimization technique. This framework enables both attackers and defenders to autonomously evolve via a Textual Gradient Optimization (TGO) module, leveraging feedback from an LLM-guided evaluation loop. We evaluate our system on a real-world assignment grading dataset of prompt injection attacks and demonstrate that our method consistently outperforms existing baselines, achieving superior robustness in both attack success and detection. Specifically, the attack success rate (ASR) reaches 1.0, representing an improvement of 0.26 over the baseline. For detection, the true positive rate (TPR) improves by 0.23 compared to the previous best work, reaching 0.84, and the true negative rate (TNR) remains comparable at 0.89. Ablation studies confirm the importance of co-evolution, gradient buffering, and multi-objective optimization. We also confirm that this framework is effective in different LLMs. Our results highlight the promise of adversarial training as a scalable and effective approach for guarding prompt injections."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00088","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:24:12Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:24:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:37:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qitao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Qitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoying","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Xiaoying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Guoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzi","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Lingzi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiwen","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Caiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jundong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jundong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoming","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Xiaoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoyi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Shaoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Geng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Geng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"End-to-End On-Device Quantization-Aware Training for LLMs at Inference Cost"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-21T01:18:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:00:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-29T16:45:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09-30T02:16:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantization is an effective technique to reduce the deployment cost of large language models (LLMs), and post-training quantization (PTQ) has been widely studied due to its efficiency. However, existing PTQ methods are limited by their inability to fine-tune model parameters and often suffer significant accuracy loss in low-bit scenarios. Quantization-aware training (QAT) provides a more principled solution, but its reliance on backpropagation incurs prohibitive memory costs, limiting its practicality for LLM deployment. To address these challenges, we propose ZeroQAT, a zeroth-order optimization-based QAT framework that supports both weight and activation quantization. ZeroQAT leverages forward-only gradient estimation to eliminate backpropagation, substantially reducing computational and memory overhead while retaining the benefits of end-to-end optimization. We further introduce a lightweight variant of ZeroQAT for quantized fine-tuning, which freezes and pre-quantizes most parameters to further cut memory usage. Experiments show that ZeroQAT consistently outperforms representative PTQ and QAT baselines while requiring significantly less memory. For example, ZeroQAT enables fine-tuning of a 13B model at extremely low bit-widths (e.g., 2-4 bits) on a single 8GB GPU, and even allows fine-tuning a 6.7B model on a OnePlus 12 smartphone, demonstrating its practicality for end-to-end QAT on resource-limited edge devices."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:22:51Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:22:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-30T05:22:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2509.00039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2509.00039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanguang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chuanguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengtao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Zhengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeyu","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zeyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hansheng","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Hansheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhulin","familyName":"An","name":"An, Zhulin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingli","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Yingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AMMKD: Adaptive Multimodal Multi-teacher Distillation for Lightweight Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-08-23T04:52:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09-03T00:00:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-09","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The success of large-scale visual language pretraining (VLP) models has driven widespread adoption of image-text retrieval tasks. However, their deployment on mobile devices remains limited due to large model sizes and computational complexity. We propose Adaptive Multi-Modal Multi-Teacher Knowledge Distillation (AMMKD), a novel framework that integrates multi-modal feature fusion, multi-teacher distillation, and adaptive optimization to deliver lightweight yet effective retrieval models. Specifically, our method begins with a feature fusion network that extracts and merges discriminative features from both the image and text modalities. To reduce model parameters and further improve performance, we design a multi-teacher knowledge distillation framework to pre-train two CLIP teacher models. We decouple modalities by pre-computing and storing text features as class vectors via the teacher text encoder to enhance efficiency. To better align teacher and student outputs, we apply KL scatter for probability distribution matching. Finally, we design an adaptive dynamic weighting scheme that treats multi-teacher distillation as a multi-objective optimization problem. By leveraging gradient space diversity, we dynamically adjust the influence of each teacher, reducing conflicts and guiding the student toward more optimal learning directions. Extensive experiments on three benchmark datasets demonstrate that AMMKD achieves superior performance while significantly reducing model complexity, validating its effectiveness and flexibility."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.00039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-09-03T03:23:02Z","registered":"2025-09-03T03:23:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-09-03T03:23:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":5,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=(doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00031%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00039%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00072%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.00088%20OR%20doi%3A10.48550%2Farxiv.2509.04467)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=10"}}